## 24 July 2021 — the day the world said "yes"

On that day the UNESCO World Heritage Committee, meeting at its extended 44th session chaired from Fuzhou, China, inscribed **"The Great Spa Towns of Europe"** on the World Heritage List by decision **44 COM 8B.16**. The property received the number 1613, and Mariánské Lázně (Marienbad) is listed within it as component 1613-005 — one of eleven towns granted the highest level of international protection for cultural heritage.

It was no accident. It was the result of a decade of preparation, expert documentation and international cooperation between seven European states: the first impulse came from a conference in Baden-Baden in 2010, the nomination dossier of more than a thousand pages was submitted to UNESCO in January 2019, and on 4 June 2021 the advisory body ICOMOS published its recommendation to inscribe.

## What are the Great Spa Towns of Europe?

**The Great Spa Towns of Europe** is a transnational serial UNESCO property that recognises the exceptional European spa phenomenon — a cultural phenomenon that peaked from around 1700 to the 1930s.

It is not a list of individual buildings. It is the recognition of **an entire system** — the interlinking of natural resources, architecture, town planning, medicine, culture and social life that made the spa towns of Europe a civilisational phenomenon in their own right.

